\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Compatible volumes for real Fourier spaces}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Let a finite Abelian translation group act orthogonally on a finite real inner-product space #{E}. In the complexification let #{V_r} be the character space at #{r}; conjugation exchanges #{V_r} and #{V_{-r}}. At a self-opposite character put #{E_r=E\cap V_r}. For an unordered pair #{o=\{r,-r\}} of distinct labels put #{E_o=E\cap(V_r\oplus V_{-r})}. Choose one representative #{r} in each pair. Its complex structure #{J_o} acts after complexification by #{\ui} on #{V_r} and #{-\ui} on #{V_{-r}}. This action preserves the original real space and is orthogonal.}

\p{Assume the self-opposite summands have even real dimensions. Choose a unit real top covolume on each. On #{E_o} use the unit covolume of its complex orientation, with each unit vector followed by its #{J_o}-image in an orthonormal complex frame. These factor volumes are \newvocab{compatible Fourier volumes} for the product covolume #{\omega} on #{E}: their exterior product is #{\omega}. Every factor dimension is even, so the order of the summands leaves this product unchanged. The scalar \vocabk{Pfaffian}{fdip-008I} refers to the indicated volume.}
